UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 4: Build suspense with a fair clue', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"Gentle suspense gives readers a clear goal, a fair clue and a reveal that answers the planted question without cruelty or gore.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same strategy to an unfamiliar topic or audience, then compare the effect of two different choices.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nPlan a missing-key story: state the goal, one fair clue and a reveal that uses the clue. Name one detail you would delay.\n\nFEEDBACK\nGoal + clue + connected reveal; delay the answer, not every useful fact about who wants the key.\n\nCHECK\nWithholding every fact leaves readers confused; inventing a random twist that ignores the clue breaks trust.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nWrite a 140–180 word original suspense scene with a clear goal, one fair clue, controlled pace and a reveal that answers the clue. Underline the clue and explain one pacing choice.\n\nWrite a short reflection describing one specific revision you made and why.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"English","learning_intention":"Gentle suspense gives readers a clear goal, a fair clue and a reveal that answers the planted question without cruelty or gore.","lesson_number":2,"lesson_role":"Writing","materials":["Paper or typed answer space","Pencil"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper and pencil to complete the original task, then retain a photograph or short parent observation as evidence.","outcome_codes":["EN2-CWT-01"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: Write a 140–180 word original suspense scene with a clear goal, one fair clue, controlled pace and a reveal that answers the clue. Underline the clue and explain one pacing choice. Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Original response on new material: Goal + clue + connected reveal; delay the answer, not every useful fact about who wants the key. Reasoning: I used suspense by attending to clue rather than copying the worked model.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Common error: Withholding every fact leaves readers confused; inventing a random twist that ignores the clue breaks trust. Correction: Goal + clue + connected reveal; delay the answer, not every useful fact about who wants the key. Why: the correction restores accurate use of suspense.\n\nParent: Likely misconception: Withholding every fact leaves readers confused; inventing a random twist that ignores the clue breaks trust. Keep sample answers out of the child role.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 1: Produce a fresh example demonstrating suspense on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 2: Name one likely error with suspense, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.\n\nParent: Marking notes: Require independently produced work with a specific supporting detail. / Require misconception + correction + purpose-linked reason..","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Goal + clue + connected reveal; delay the answer, not every useful fact about who wants the key. This matches the lesson skill for suspense.","options":["Ignore the words and invent a more exciting event.","Copy the model word-for-word and call that independent work.","Goal + clue + connected reveal; delay the answer, not every useful fact about who wants the key.","Withholding every fact leaves readers confused; inventing a random twist that ignores the clue breaks trust."],"question":"Plan a missing-key story: state the goal, one fair clue and a reveal that uses the clue. Name one detail you would delay. Which response is most accurate?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Withholding every fact leaves readers confused; inventing a random twist that ignores the clue breaks trust. That misconception weakens accuracy; the other choices align with the lesson idea.","options":["Goal + clue + connected reveal; delay the answer, not every useful fact about who wants the key.","Careful use of clue with clear evidence.","Withholding every fact leaves readers confused; inventing a random twist that ignores the clue breaks trust.","Gentle suspense gives readers a clear goal, a fair clue and a reveal that answers the planted question without cruelty or gore."],"question":"Which statement is a common mistake when practising suspense?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Understanding is shown by new evidence and named clue, not by copying the model.","options":["Choose randomly because any option could be right.","Treat a missing detail as a stated fact.","Apply the idea with new evidence and name the clue used.","Repeat the worked example wording with no new evidence."],"question":"A learner is practising suspense. Which approach best shows understanding?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"The skill is shown by quoting or pointing to wording that carries suspense.","options":["Ignore the sentence and invent a more exciting event.","Say the writing is good without naming any words.","Withholding every fact leaves readers confused; inventing a random twist that ignores the clue breaks trust.","Point to exact words in the sentence that show suspense and say what they do."],"question":"Read: \"In the dusty shed, Lila oiled the bike chain before the race.\" Which choice best uses suspense from this lesson?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"The correct statement applies the language skill; the others miss the evidence.","options":["Copy the model word-for-word and call that independent work.","Goal + clue + connected reveal; delay the answer, not every useful fact about who wants the key.","Withholding every fact leaves readers confused; inventing a random twist that ignores the clue breaks trust.","Ignore the words and invent a more exciting event."],"question":"Which statement correctly applies the lesson idea about suspense?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Start from a specific textual clue, then explain its effect.","options":["Locate a specific word, phrase or structural clue before deciding.","Guess from the title alone with no textual clue.","Copy a model sentence from another story unchanged.","Treat a missing detail as a stated fact."],"question":"In a new text, what should you do first to practise clue?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Repair means fixing meaning or evidence, not padding.","options":["Change the inaccurate idea or missing evidence, not only the length.","Add adjectives without fixing the wrong idea.","Make the answer longer while keeping the same mistake.","Delete the evidence that disagreed with your first guess."],"question":"Which revision actually repairs meaning?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Transfer needs fresh material with an explained language choice.","options":["Use a fresh sentence or text and explain one precise choice about suspense.","Retype the worked model as the final answer.","Change fonts and claim the work is new.","Withholding every fact leaves readers confused; inventing a random twist that ignores the clue breaks trust."],"question":"Which response shows transfer of suspense to new material?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Independent evidence on new material demonstrates the skill; copying the model does not.","marking_guide":"Require independently produced work with a specific supporting detail.","question":"Produce a fresh example demonstrating suspense on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.","sample_answer":"Original response on new material: Goal + clue + connected reveal; delay the answer, not every useful fact about who wants the key. Reasoning: I used suspense by attending to clue rather than copying the worked model.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A useful correction changes the inaccurate idea or method and explains why.","marking_guide":"Require misconception + correction + purpose-linked reason.","question":"Name one likely error with suspense, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.","sample_answer":"Common error: Withholding every fact leaves readers confused; inventing a random twist that ignores the clue breaks trust. Correction: Goal + clue + connected reveal; delay the answer, not every useful fact about who wants the key. Why: the correction restores accurate use of suspense.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent task and reflection, with explanations where requested.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s2-eng-y4-w01-l2","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Stage 2 NESA alignment; verify content points before acceptance.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S2","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise suspense.","Read the mission before you start writing or speaking.","Keep the audience and purpose in mind as you work."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your reason with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not match the evidence."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the wording that carries the meaning.","Then explain the choice without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore 