bel before visitors arrive. A rustle behind the board is a fair clue. Reveal: her little brother hid a thank-you card, knocking the label. The clue connects to the answer; pacing holds the question for two short beats.\n\nTRY FIRST\nPlan a missing-key story: state the goal, one fair clue and a reveal that uses the clue. Name one detail you would delay.\n\nFEEDBACK\nGoal + clue + connected reveal; delay the answer, not every useful fact about who wants the key.","explain":"Gentle suspense gives readers a clear goal, a fair clue and a reveal that answers the planted question without cruelty or gore.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make decisions based on specific language evidence, rather than guess or copy someone else. Keep the meaning accurate and explain the choice.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the original example and locate the words or actions that support the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Check your response against the feedback: did you address the same skill and show your reasoning? If a detail is uncertain, identify what would have to be checked. One deliberate, justified revision is more useful than making the answer longer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Withholding every fact leaves readers confused; inventing a random twist that ignores the clue breaks trust. Correct the meaning, not just the appearance.","title":"Understand and model","visual":[{"alt":"Suspense needs a goal and a fair clue.","caption":"Hold the answer briefly, not every fact.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Goal</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">find the label in time</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Clue</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\